{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "📱 Tour 5: Capacitor Hybrid Mobile Bridge",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "scripture-habit/capacitor.config.ts",
      "line": 3,
      "description": "### 📱 Capacitor Bridge Config\n\nCapacitor wraps our Vite React application (`dist/`) inside a native WebView wrapper. Here we define the unique App Bundle ID (`com.scripturehabit.app`), App Name, and point `webDir` to Vite's output folder."
    },
    {
      "file": "scripture-habit/capacitor.config.ts",
      "line": 7,
      "description": "### 🔑 Native Google OAuth Plugin\n\nFor seamless native logins, we configure the `GoogleAuth` plugin. This enables native Google sign-in prompts on Android and iOS devices, returning OAuth tokens back to our Firebase Authentication handler."
    },
    {
      "file": "scripture-habit/src/utils/storage.ts",
      "line": 4,
      "description": "### 💾 Safe Local Storage & Cache Boundaries\n\nMobile WebViews can occasionally clear cache or run in restricted modes. `safeStorage` is a custom wrapper with defensive try-catch guards to handle storage failures, ensuring the app remains fully functional without crashing!"
    },
    {
      "file": "scripture-habit/capacitor.config.ts",
      "line": 6,
      "description": "### 🚀 WebView LiveReload Workflow\n\nDuring local mobile development, Capacitor can route directly to Vite's local dev server (`http://[local-ip]:5173`) by setting the `server.url` property in this config. This enables Hot Module Replacement (HMR) to reflect changes immediately on actual connected phones!"
    }
  ]
}
